the mass distribution function explicit. I'll use source 2 as the primary reference since it includes that useful intermediate step from source 3.\n\nNow I'm moving to analyze the second slot of sources, which all have high confidence ratings and need to be deduplicated and merged together. Now I'm mapping the citation tokens to their corresponding keys—Baker2013 for the Poisson AEP reference in source 3, and I need to figure out how to handle the KB document citation since I don't have complete metadata for it. I'll use a generic knowledge base key for that internal reference. For the KB document without full metadata, I'll assign the citekey `HazardKB` to `[KB:hazard.md]`, while the other citations like `Baker2013`, `Kramer1996`, `McGuire1995`, and `BazzurroCornell1999` are already in proper citekey format and can be mapped directly.\n\nNow I'm structuring the canonical answer by pulling the most comprehensive version from source 2 and weaving in the structural clarity from source 3, since source 1 largely overlaps with both. I need to cover the introductory framing, the general hazard integral equation, then walk through each of the four main components: the annual occurrence rate, the magnitude distribution, the distance distribution for a circular source, and the conditional exceedance probability. Epsilon reformulation (tail integral identity)\n8. Assembly: final factored epsilon form\n\nSource 2 has all these. Source 3 also has all these but in slightly different wording. I'll use source 2's structure as primary with source 3's additional step showing the intermediate form:\n\nFrom source 3:\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*) = \\frac{2\\nu_0}{R^2} \\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}} \\int_{R_{\\min}}^{R_{\\max}} r\\; f_M(m) \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon\\; dr\\, dm$$\n\nThis intermediate form (with $f_M(m)$ explicit) is distinct from source 2's final fully-expanded form. I want to show both versions to illustrate how substituting $f_R(r)$ first leads into the complete expansion with $f_M(m)$.\n\nSource 2 is the most comprehensive for the canonical answer, but source 3 contributes the multi-year exceedance probability formula $P_{T_R}[I > i^*] = 1 - \\exp[-\\lambda_I(i^*) T_R]$, the M-R bin rate in its notation, and the closed-form evaluation of the epsilon integral. I'll merge these together into one cohesive presentation. I'm comparing how source 2 handles the 3D disaggregation with its binned epsilon approach versus source 3's cleaner separation of that integral as its own equation. I'll go with source 2 as the foundation but adopt source 3's structure for clarity, and I need to set up the output using the session compiler convention.\n\nFor the coordination between slots, slot 1 will establish the full hazard framework with all its components and the epsilon reformulation, while slot 2 can build on that foundation to develop the Poisson AEP relation and disaggregation methodology without repeating the derivations. Now I'm converting the citation format from the knowledge base references to the standard bibliography format. I'm mapping out the structure for slot 1, pulling the most detailed explanation from source 2 and filling in source 3's intermediate epsilon reformulation step. The key sections flow from the introductory setup through the hazard integral components, then converge on that unique intermediate form showing the factored structure before substitution, and finally the fully expanded version. uting $f_M(m)$.\n\nLet me write this now.\n\nFor the intro paragraph, I'll fuse sources 2 and 3:\n- Source 2: \"The configuration examined is a stable continental crust region in which seismicity is represented by a single circular areal source...\"\n- Source 3: \"The particularization of the general PSHA hazard integral to the single circular areal source begins by identifying four components...\"\n\nI'll use source 3's structure (starts with the particularization approach) which is cleaner, and add the configuration details from source 2.\n\nActually source 2's intro has more specific characterization but source 3's is more precise for the task. Let me fuse:\n\nThe general PSHA hazard integral is particularized to a single circular areal source of radius $R$ centered on the study site, with spatially uniform seismicity and no contributions from sources beyond radius $R$. The source is characterized by Gutenberg-Richter parameters $a$ and $b$, engineering minimum magnitude $M_{\\min}$, and maximum magnitude $M_{\\max}$. Four components of the integrand require explicit specification: the annual occurrence rate $\\nu_0$, the truncated Gutenberg-Richter magnitude probability density function $f_M(m)$, the conditional ground-motion exceedance probability $P[I > i^* \\mid m, r]$, and the distance probability density function $f_R(r)$ appropriate to the circular source geometry. I'm treating site-intensity $I$ as lognormally distributed with median $\\hat{\\eta}_I(m, r)$ and logarithmic standard deviation $\\sigma_{\\ln I}$ under reference rock conditions, without assuming any specific ground motion prediction equation. Now I'm organizing the document structure—I'll use source 2 as the foundation and weave in source 3's multi-year probability framework and the epsilon bin integral, while setting aside source 1 since it overlaps with the other two. I need to clean up all the confidence footnotes, standardize the citation format, and apply consistent formatting rules across both slots before composing the final output. Source 3 breaks down the same setup into four key components of the hazard integral: the annual occurrence rate, the magnitude distribution, the ground-motion exceedance probability, and the distance distribution for the circular geometry, with the same source characterization parameters and uniform seismicity assumption. The ground-motion intensity follows a lognormal distribution with median $\\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)$ and log standard deviation $\\sigma_{\\ln I}$ under reference rock conditions. For the single circular source, I'm setting up the particularized hazard integral that integrates over magnitude and distance, with the annual exceedance rate expressed as a function of the four key components: occurrence rate, magnitude distribution, ground-motion exceedance probability, and distance distribution for the circular geometry.\n\nNow I'm working through the first component—the annual occurrence rate derived from the Gutenberg-Richter recurrence relation, which gives the mean annual number of earthquakes at or above a given magnitude.\n\nFor the second component, I need to establish the magnitude probability density function by normalizing the Gutenberg-Richter relation over the engineering magnitude range, which yields a closed-form truncated distribution.\n\nNow moving to the third component, I'm deriving the distance probability density function for a circular source with uniform seismicity, where the probability of an earthquake occurring at distance $r$ depends on the area of the annular ring at that distance. distance $r$ is given by a lognormal distribution formula that uses the standard normal CDF to compute the probability that intensity exceeds a target threshold, with the logarithmic residual quantifying how many standard deviations above the median prediction the target lies.\n\nNow I'm reformulating this using the tail integral of the standard normal probability density function instead of the complementary CDF, which provides an equivalent but more useful representation for the subsequent derivation.\n\nSubstituting this epsilon representation into the hazard integral transforms it into a triple integral over magnitude, distance, and epsilon, where the joint density of magnitude and distance weights the contribution from the standard normal tail at each point in the parameter space. This makes the convolution structure explicit—for any given magnitude-distance pair, I'm integrating the weighted normal density over the exceedance region.\n\nWith the circular source distance distribution and the epsilon tail-integral substitution in place, I can now write out the particularized annual exceedance rate in its epsilon form.\n\nApplying the truncated Gutenberg-Richter magnitude distribution and pulling all constants outside the triple integral gives me the final expression for the annual exceedance rate. The integration limits are defined by the source geometry and magnitude range, with the critical threshold $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$ depending on both magnitude and distance through the ground motion prediction equation. Since this dependence prevents analytical solutions, I need to rely on numerical integration over the magnitude-distance rectangle. This hazard integral then feeds into two main applications for a circular areal source.\n\nThe first is determining the Maximum Considered Earthquake intensity, and the second is seismic hazard disaggregation—both rely on the normalized standard normal distribution and the residual threshold $\\varepsilon^*(m, r)$ that compares the target intensity to the predicted median. For the MCE determination specifically, I'm connecting the annual exceedance probability to the exceedance rate under Poisson occurrence, which relates how often ground motions exceed a specified intensity level. The MCE intensity is found by numerically inverting the hazard curve to solve for the intensity corresponding to the target exceedance rate. For a conditional scenario with maximum magnitude and distance, I'm working through the epsilon-space formulation to express the exceedance probability in terms of the standard normal distribution. periods and increases with return period, which makes sense since rarer ground motions correspond to larger positive residuals from the median prediction. For the representative distance, I can either use the geometric mean of the epicentral distance distribution—which works out to two-thirds of the maximum source radius—or select the modal magnitude-distance pair from disaggregation analysis, whichever is more appropriate for the application.\n\nNow I'm moving into hazard disaggregation, which breaks down the total annual exceedance rate into contributions from discrete magnitude-distance-residual bins. For each scenario, I need to calculate the critical residual threshold that determines whether that particular magnitude-distance pair can produce the target intensity level, then identify which scenarios actually contribute to exceeding that intensity.\n\nFor the two-dimensional magnitude-distance disaggregation, I'm choosing between two equivalent notational approaches and settling on the form that factors out the geometric terms more explicitly. The bin rate for each magnitude-distance pair comes from integrating over the residual distribution. The weights sum to one by construction, and the modal scenario identifies the magnitude-distance bin with the highest contribution to hazard at the given intensity level. Both the bin-level and total exceedance rates require numerical integration for realistic ground motion prediction equations. Now I'm extending this to full three-dimensional disaggregation by including the epsilon dimension, where each bin spans a range of residuals and contributes its own exceedance rate. The closed-form solution for the epsilon integral simplifies things considerably—when the upper bin boundary exceeds the threshold, I get the difference between two cumulative distribution evaluations, otherwise it's zero. This reduces the original three-dimensional integral down to a two-dimensional problem over mass and radius that I'll need to solve numerically for realistic ground motion prediction equations.\n\nNow I'm working through the conditional probability framework for disaggregation. Each bin's contribution to the total hazard is defined as the ratio of its hazard rate to the overall intensity hazard rate, which automatically satisfies the normalization condition. From this joint distribution, I can derive marginal probabilities by summing across the other dimensions to isolate the contribution of individual parameters like magnitude.\n\nFor the circular source geometry, there's a fundamental tension: the distance probability density function pushes probability toward larger epicentral distances, but ground motion attenuation reduces the likelihood of exceeding the intensity threshold as distance increases. The marginal conditional distribution for distance reflects how these competing effects balance out. I'm looking up the Baker citation more carefully—there's a 2008 version and a 2013 update, both widely used references on PSHA methodology. Now I'm finishing up the McGuire reference and moving on to the Bazzurro and Cornell paper on seismic hazard disaggregation. For the internal knowledge base document, I'll create a miscellaneous entry since I don't have complete bibliographic details, treating it as a project-specific reference on PSHA methodology.\n\nWriting BibLaTeX entries... I'm continuing to compile the bibliography entries for seismic hazard analysis references, adding the publication details for the Kramer textbook and then moving through key journal articles on probabilistic seismic hazard methods and disaggregation techniques.\n\nNow I'm working through the Baker document and deciding on the appropriate BibTeX entry type—since it's a tutorial document with a URL, I'll use @misc rather than @techreport. For the HazardKB entry, I'm being careful not to fabricate missing information like author or year, so I'll keep it minimal with just the title and note that it's an internal document.\n\nI'm ready to generate the final output files: the two slot markdown files and the compiled research document, along with the bibliography file, all organized relative to the session directory. I'm checking the format requirements—ASCII text only, straight quotes and standard hyphens, maintaining an academic tone throughout. The content already follows these rules with LaTeX equations and proper citation formatting. Now I need to verify which contributions are unique to each slot from the earlier session, since slot 1 and 2 both reference the same source material but may have slightly different phrasings or structural variations I should account for. Looking at source 2 more carefully, I see it includes the multi-year probability formula, MCE epsilon inversion, and the mean distance calculation, along with the M-R disaggregation and full 3D treatment with the epsilon bounds applied directly in the bin rate. Source 3 for the same slot has the same multi-year probability and MCE structure but appears to handle the M-R binning differently.\n\nNow examining how source 2 formulates the 3D disaggregation—it integrates over magnitude and distance with the epsilon bounds (both upper and lower, accounting for the maximum with the completeness threshold) applied directly within the bin rate expression, whereas source 3 splits this into separate equations for the bin rate and the inner integral over epsilon. I'm checking the formatting requirements now—making sure to use ASCII text only, no smart quotes, and keeping everything in plain text format. The author names and mathematical notation are all compatible with these constraints. Let me start writing out the four files in sequence. and contributions from beyond radius $R$ are negligible; the distance integration domain reduces to $[0, R]$. The source is characterized by Gutenberg-Richter parameters $a$ and $b$, engineering minimum magnitude $M_{\\min}$, and maximum magnitude $M_{\\max}$. Four components of the integrand require explicit specification: the annual occurrence rate $\\nu_0$, the truncated Gutenberg-Richter magnitude probability density function $f_M(m)$, the conditional ground-motion exceedance probability $P[I > i^* \\mid m, r]$, and the distance probability density function $f_R(r)$ appropriate to the circular source geometry. No specific ground motion prediction equation is assumed; the ground-motion intensity $I$ is treated as lognormally distributed with median $\\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)$ and logarithmic standard deviation $\\sigma_{\\ln I}$ under reference rock conditions.\n\nFor a single circular source, the annual exceedance rate becomes a double integral over magnitude and distance, where the magnitude ranges from $M_{\\min}$ to $M_{\\max}$ and distance from the source center to the site ranges from 0 to $R$. This integral combines the occurrence rate, magnitude distribution, ground-motion exceedance probability, and distance distribution. Now I'm setting up the individual components, starting with how earthquake magnitudes recur according to the Gutenberg-Richter relation.\n\nThe annual occurrence rate comes from evaluating the Gutenberg-Richter recurrence relation at the engineering minimum magnitude, giving the mean number of seismically relevant events per year that the source produces. The magnitude probability density is then obtained by normalizing the Gutenberg-Richter distribution over the engineering magnitude range.\n\nWorking through the normalization yields a closed-form expression for the truncated Gutenberg-Richter PDF, where the denominator serves as the normalization constant that ensures the density integrates to one across the valid magnitude interval. Now I'm moving to the distance distribution for the circular source model.\n\nFor a site at the center of a circular areal source with uniform seismicity, the probability density for earthquake distance follows from the ratio of annular ring area to total disk area, giving a distribution that increases monotonically with distance—a geometric consequence of having more source area at larger epicentral distances. The uniform seismicity assumption also implies that magnitude and location are independent, so the conditional distance distribution given magnitude is identical to the marginal distance distribution.\n\nNow I'm moving to the conditional exceedance probability, which uses a lognormal model to compute the probability that ground motion intensity exceeds a target threshold given magnitude and distance. The calculation involves the standard normal CDF applied to a logarithmic residual, which measures how many standard deviations the target intensity lies from the median predicted intensity.\n\nThe epsilon reformulation is the key insight here—replacing the complementary normal CDF with the tail integral of the standard normal probability density function, which enables a more efficient computational approach. is exact for all finite values of the threshold [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$1 - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\right) = \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon$$\n\nSubstituting this representation converts the two-dimensional hazard integral into a triple integral over the domain $(m, r, \\varepsilon)$, with $\\varepsilon$ integrated from the scenario-dependent lower limit $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$ to $+\\infty$. This formulation makes explicit the convolution structure of the hazard integral: for each $(m, r)$ pair the contribution to $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ is a weighted integral of the standard normal PDF $\\phi(\\varepsilon)$ over the exceedance region, with the joint kernel $f_M(m) \\cdot f_R(r)$ providing the weighting. [@HazardKB]\n\nNow I'm assembling the particularized annual exceedance rate by substituting the epsilon tail-integral representation and the circular source distance PDF $f_R(r) = 2r/R^2$ into the double integral, which yields the triple integral form:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*) = \\frac{2\\nu_0}{R^2} \\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}} \\int_{R_{\\min}}^{R_{\\max}} r\\; f_M(m) \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon\\, dr\\, dm$$\n\nNow I'm substituting the truncated Gutenberg-Richter density for $f_M(m)$ and factoring out all constant terms to get the final form of the annual exceedance rate in epsilon space, where the constants in front of the triple integral are determined by the source characteristics and the magnitude-frequency distribution.\n\nThe integrand itself represents a convolution between the standard normal PDF and the Gutenberg-Richter distribution, weighted by the geometric distance factor $r$, with integration bounds across magnitude, distance, and intensity, where the lower intensity limit $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$ varies with both magnitude and distance. Now I'm defining the critical residual threshold that determines when ground motion exceeds the target intensity, then setting up the relationship between annual exceedance probability and return period under Poisson assumptions to solve for the Maximum Considered Earthquake.\n\nFor a given return period, I can invert the exceedance probability formula to find the corresponding annual rate, using the standard engineering approximation that simplifies the logarithmic term for large return periods—this implicitly defines the MCE intensity as the ground motion level that satisfies this target rate.\n\nSince the full hazard integral requires numerical evaluation for realistic ground motion prediction equations, finding the MCE intensity also demands numerical inversion of the hazard curve. For the MCE scenario conditioned on maximum magnitude and distance, I'm setting up the conditional exceedance probability in terms of the standard normal variable epsilon, then equating it to the target annual exceedance probability and inverting to get the critical epsilon level that determines the MCE ground motion.\n\nFrom this inversion, I get closed-form expressions for both the critical epsilon value and the corresponding MCE intensity—the epsilon term scales with the return period logarithmically through the inverse normal CDF, while the intensity itself combines the median GMPE prediction with a lognormal scaling factor. The critical epsilon is always positive for practical return periods and increases with longer return periods, which makes physical sense since rarer events correspond to ground motions above the median prediction. The choice of representative distance still needs to be addressed.\n\nFor the distance parameter, I can either use the geometric mean of the circular source distribution—integrating the distance-weighted PDF over the source radius gives $\\bar{r} = \\frac{2R}{3}$—or I can pull the modal magnitude-distance pair directly from hazard disaggregation results, which would be more consistent with the actual hazard drivers. Either way, the choice needs to be documented clearly.\n\nNow I'm moving into hazard disaggregation, which breaks down the total exceedance rate into contributions from individual scenario bins.\n\nFor each magnitude-distance pair, there's a critical residual threshold that determines whether that scenario can produce the target intensity level. The residual threshold depends on how far the scenario's median prediction falls below the target intensity—scenarios requiring large positive residuals to reach the target contribute very little to the overall hazard.\n\nNow I'm setting up the two-dimensional disaggregation by integrating over magnitude and distance bins. The bin rate accounts for the spatial density of sources and the probability that the ground motion residual exceeds the critical threshold needed for exceedance. From this, I can compute the fractional contribution of each magnitude-distance pair to the total hazard. The three-dimensional disaggregation extends this to include the GMPE residual term, where the contribution from a magnitude-distance-epsilon bin is computed through a triple integral over the magnitude and distance ranges with the epsilon integral evaluated over the standard normal distribution. The epsilon component admits a closed-form solution using the standard normal CDF. The closed-form result lets me reduce this to a double integral over magnitude and distance for each bin, which I'll need to evaluate numerically for realistic ground motion models. Then I'm computing the joint conditional probability that an earthquake falls into bin $(m_k, r_j, \\varepsilon_\\ell)$ given that it exceeds the intensity threshold, normalized by the total exceedance rate—this automatically satisfies the completeness condition across all bins.\n\nFrom there, I can extract the marginal distributions by collapsing over the other dimensions: summing across distance and residual bins gives the magnitude distribution, summing across magnitude and residual gives the distance distribution, and so on. For a circular source geometry, the distance probability density concentrates toward larger radii according to the geometric weighting $2r/R^2$.\n\nHowever, this geometric effect competes with the GMPE attenuation, which suppresses exceedance probability as distance increases. The resulting marginal conditional distribution for distance typically peaks at some intermediate distance rather than at the extremes, and finding that peak requires numerical computation for the specific GMPE being used. I'm adding more foundational references on seismic hazard analysis, including McGuire's work on closing the loop between hazard analysis and design earthquakes, and Bazzurro and Cornell's key paper on disaggregating seismic hazard to understand which earthquakes contribute most to overall risk.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17651,"cache_read_input_tokens":41926,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":17651,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aaa7868a-6dc9-476b-8d17-65e6c005c5fe","uuid":"023e6217-98d5-447a-9f92-a2c04a25258f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01KMNfGKCuGD3m55BmwNWBRM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"SLOT_PROGRESS: slot-01 :: writing_output\nSLOT_PROGRESS: slot-02 :: writing_output\n\nWriting both slot output files in parallel:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17651,"cache_read_input_tokens":41926,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":17651,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aaa7868a-6dc9-476b-8d17-65e6c005c5fe","uuid":"250c1ec4-56bc-4f04-9d80-5541a9a82f4b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01KMNfGKCuGD3m55BmwNWBRM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01F4Mu5sTBAkByy4pUytwe2z","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/pshaB/sessions/run-20260329_152743/pshaB_test2.compile.research.slot-01.md","content":"## SLOT 1: Derivation of the particularized annual exceedance rate in epsilon form\n\nThe general PSHA hazard integral is particularized to a single circular areal source of radius $R$ centered on the study site. Seismicity within the source is spatially uniform, and contributions from beyond radius $R$ are negligible; the distance integration domain reduces to $[0, R]$. The source is characterized by Gutenberg-Richter parameters $a$ and $b$, engineering minimum magnitude $M_{\\min}$, and maximum magnitude $M_{\\max}$. Four components of the integrand require explicit specification: the annual occurrence rate $\\nu_0$, the truncated Gutenberg-Richter magnitude probability density function $f_M(m)$, the conditional ground-motion exceedance probability $P[I > i^* \\mid m, r]$, and the distance probability density function $f_R(r)$ appropriate to the circular source geometry. No specific ground motion prediction equation is assumed; the ground-motion intensity $I$ is treated as lognormally distributed with median $\\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)$ and logarithmic standard deviation $\\sigma_{\\ln I}$ under reference rock conditions throughout. [@Baker2013][@Kramer1996][@HazardKB]\n\nReducing the general multi-source hazard sum to the single circular source, the particularized annual exceedance rate takes the form [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*) = \\nu_0 \\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}} \\int_{R_{\\min}}^{R_{\\max}} P\\!\\left[I > i^* \\mid m, r\\right] f_M(m)\\, f_R(r)\\, dr\\, dm$$\n\nwhere $R_{\\min} = 0$ and $R_{\\max} = R$ are the inner and outer radial limits imposed by the circular source geometry centered on the site. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (i): Annual occurrence rate.** The Gutenberg-Richter recurrence relation $\\log_{10} N(M) = a - bM$, where $N(M)$ is the mean annual number of earthquakes at or above magnitude $M$, is evaluated at the engineering minimum magnitude $M_{\\min}$ to yield the annual occurrence rate [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\nu_0 = 10^{a - b\\,M_{\\min}}$$\n\nThe parameters $a$ and $b$ are the activity rate and slope of the Gutenberg-Richter relation for the source. The rate $\\nu_0$ counts the mean annual number of seismically relevant events on the source and is factored outside all subsequent integrals. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (ii): Truncated Gutenberg-Richter magnitude PDF.** The magnitude density is obtained by normalizing the Gutenberg-Richter density over the engineering support $[M_{\\min}, M_{\\max}]$. Evaluating the normalization denominator and canceling common factors yields the closed-form truncated Gutenberg-Richter PDF [@Kramer1996][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$f_M(m) = \\frac{b\\ln 10\\; 10^{-b(m - M_{\\min})}}{1 - 10^{-b(M_{\\max}-M_{\\min})}}, \\qquad M_{\\min} \\leq m \\leq M_{\\max}$$\n\nThe denominator $1 - 10^{-b(M_{\\max}-M_{\\min})}$ is the normalization constant ensuring that $f_M(m)$ integrates to unity over $[M_{\\min}, M_{\\max}]$. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (iii): Distance PDF for the circular source.** For a site at the center of a circular areal source with spatially uniform seismicity, the probability that an earthquake originates within the annular ring at distance $r$ from the site is proportional to the ring area $2\\pi r\\,dr$ divided by the total disk area $\\pi R^2$, yielding [@Baker2013][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$f_R(r) = \\frac{2r}{R^2}, \\qquad 0 \\leq r \\leq R$$\n\nThe density is monotonically increasing in $r$, reflecting the geometric fact that more source area lies at larger epicentral distances from the central site. Under the uniform seismicity assumption, earthquake locations are spatially independent of earthquake magnitude, so the conditional distance distribution given magnitude reduces to the unconditional form: $f_{R|M}(r|m) = f_R(r)$. This independence property carries through the full magnitude-distance integral without modification. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (iv): Conditional exceedance probability.** The conditional probability that intensity $I$ exceeds target level $i^*$ given magnitude $m$ and distance $r$ is [@Kramer1996][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$P\\!\\left[I > i^* \\mid m, r\\right] = 1 - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\frac{\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m, r)}{\\sigma_{\\ln I}}\\right)$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(\\cdot)$ is the standard normal cumulative distribution function. The signed logarithmic residual threshold $\\varepsilon^*(m, r)$, defined as the argument of $\\Phi$ above, quantifies the number of logarithmic standard deviations by which the target intensity $i^*$ lies above the GMPE median for scenario $(m, r)$ [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\varepsilon^*(m, r) \\equiv \\frac{\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m, r)}{\\sigma_{\\ln I}}$$\n\nWhen $i^* = \\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)$ the exceedance probability equals exactly 0.5; exceedance becomes increasingly improbable as $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$ increases. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Epsilon reformulation.** The key step in the epsilon form of the hazard integral is the replacement of the complementary standard normal CDF by the tail integral of the standard normal probability density function $\\phi(\\varepsilon) = (2\\pi)^{-1/2}\\exp(-\\varepsilon^2/2)$. This identity is exact for all finite values of the threshold [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$1 - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\right) = \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon$$\n\nSubstituting this representation converts the two-dimensional hazard integral into a triple integral over the domain $(m, r, \\varepsilon)$, with $\\varepsilon$ integrated from the scenario-dependent lower limit $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$ to $+\\infty$. This formulation makes explicit the convolution structure of the hazard integral: for each $(m, r)$ pair the contribution to $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ is a weighted integral of the standard normal PDF $\\phi(\\varepsilon)$ over the exceedance region, with the joint kernel $f_M(m) \\cdot f_R(r)$ providing the weighting. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Assembly: particularized annual exceedance rate in epsilon form.** Substituting the epsilon tail-integral representation of the exceedance probability and the circular source distance PDF $f_R(r) = 2r/R^2$ into the particularized double integral, and noting $R_{\\min} = 0$ and $R_{\\max} = R$, yields [@HazardKB][@Baker2013]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*) = \\frac{2\\nu_0}{R^2} \\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}} \\int_{R_{\\min}}^{R_{\\max}} r\\; f_M(m) \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon\\; dr\\, dm$$\n\nSubstituting the explicit truncated Gutenberg-Richter density for $f_M(m)$ and factoring all remaining constant terms outside the triple integral produces the final particularized annual exceedance rate in epsilon form [@HazardKB][@Baker2013]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*) = \\frac{2\\,\\nu_0\\, b\\ln 10}{R^2\\!\\left(1 - 10^{-b(M_{\\max}-M_{\\min})}\\right)} \\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}} \\int_{R_{\\min}}^{R_{\\max}} \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\; 10^{-b(m-M_{\\min})}\\; r\\; d\\varepsilon\\; dr\\; dm$$\n\nAll terms outside the triple integral are constants determined by the source parameters $a$, $b$, $M_{\\min}$, $M_{\\max}$, and source radius $R$, with $\\nu_0 = 10^{a - b\\,M_{\\min}}$ substituted explicitly. The core integrand constitutes a convolution of the standard normal PDF $\\phi(\\varepsilon)$ with the unnormalized Gutenberg-Richter magnitude distribution function $10^{-b(m-M_{\\min})}$, modulated by the geometric weight $r$ from the circular source distance PDF. The explicit integration limits are $R_{\\min} = 0$, $R_{\\max} = R$, $M_{\\min}$, and $M_{\\max}$; the lower limit of the $\\varepsilon$ integral, $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$, depends on both $m$ and $r$ through the GMPE median $\\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)$. For a general GMPE, this dependence prevents closed-form evaluation and numerical quadrature over the rectangle $[M_{\\min}, M_{\\max}] \\times [0, R]$ is required. [@Baker2013][@HazardKB]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17651,"cache_read_input_tokens":41926,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":17651,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aaa7868a-6dc9-476b-8d17-65e6c005c5fe","uuid":"d491de02-4a7b-4bce-b6c3-5161e774463e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F4Mu5sTBAkByy4pUytwe2z","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/pshaB/sessions/run-20260329_152743/pshaB_test2.compile.research.slot-01.md"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aaa7868a-6dc9-476b-8d17-65e6c005c5fe","uuid":"207cea2f-3f25-4764-b710-23a1579bdd63","timestamp":"2026-03-29T18:33:43.602Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/pshaB/sessions/run-20260329_152743/pshaB_test2.compile.research.slot-01.md","content":"## SLOT 1: Derivation of the particularized annual exceedance rate in epsilon form\n\nThe general PSHA hazard integral is particularized to a single circular areal source of radius $R$ centered on the study site. Seismicity within the source is spatially uniform, and contributions from beyond radius $R$ are negligible; the distance integration domain reduces to $[0, R]$. The source is characterized by Gutenberg-Richter parameters $a$ and $b$, engineering minimum magnitude $M_{\\min}$, and maximum magnitude $M_{\\max}$. Four components of the integrand require explicit specification: the annual occurrence rate $\\nu_0$, the truncated Gutenberg-Richter magnitude probability density function $f_M(m)$, the conditional ground-motion exceedance probability $P[I > i^* \\mid m, r]$, and the distance probability density function $f_R(r)$ appropriate to the circular source geometry. No specific ground motion prediction equation is assumed; the ground-motion intensity $I$ is treated as lognormally distributed with median $\\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)$ and logarithmic standard deviation $\\sigma_{\\ln I}$ under reference rock conditions throughout. [@Baker2013][@Kramer1996][@HazardKB]\n\nReducing the general multi-source hazard sum to the single circular source, the particularized annual exceedance rate takes the form [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*) = \\nu_0 \\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}} \\int_{R_{\\min}}^{R_{\\max}} P\\!\\left[I > i^* \\mid m, r\\right] f_M(m)\\, f_R(r)\\, dr\\, dm$$\n\nwhere $R_{\\min} = 0$ and $R_{\\max} = R$ are the inner and outer radial limits imposed by the circular source geometry centered on the site. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (i): Annual occurrence rate.** The Gutenberg-Richter recurrence relation $\\log_{10} N(M) = a - bM$, where $N(M)$ is the mean annual number of earthquakes at or above magnitude $M$, is evaluated at the engineering minimum magnitude $M_{\\min}$ to yield the annual occurrence rate [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\nu_0 = 10^{a - b\\,M_{\\min}}$$\n\nThe parameters $a$ and $b$ are the activity rate and slope of the Gutenberg-Richter relation for the source. The rate $\\nu_0$ counts the mean annual number of seismically relevant events on the source and is factored outside all subsequent integrals. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (ii): Truncated Gutenberg-Richter magnitude PDF.** The magnitude density is obtained by normalizing the Gutenberg-Richter density over the engineering support $[M_{\\min}, M_{\\max}]$. Evaluating the normalization denominator and canceling common factors yields the closed-form truncated Gutenberg-Richter PDF [@Kramer1996][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$f_M(m) = \\frac{b\\ln 10\\; 10^{-b(m - M_{\\min})}}{1 - 10^{-b(M_{\\max}-M_{\\min})}}, \\qquad M_{\\min} \\leq m \\leq M_{\\max}$$\n\nThe denominator $1 - 10^{-b(M_{\\max}-M_{\\min})}$ is the normalization constant ensuring that $f_M(m)$ integrates to unity over $[M_{\\min}, M_{\\max}]$. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (iii): Distance PDF for the circular source.** For a site at the center of a circular areal source with spatially uniform seismicity, the probability that an earthquake originates within the annular ring at distance $r$ from the site is proportional to the ring area $2\\pi r\\,dr$ divided by the total disk area $\\pi R^2$, yielding [@Baker2013][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$f_R(r) = \\frac{2r}{R^2}, \\qquad 0 \\leq r \\leq R$$\n\nThe density is monotonically increasing in $r$, reflecting the geometric fact that more source area lies at larger epicentral distances from the central site. Under the uniform seismicity assumption, earthquake locations are spatially independent of earthquake magnitude, so the conditional distance distribution given magnitude reduces to the unconditional form: $f_{R|M}(r|m) = f_R(r)$. This independence property carries through the full magnitude-distance integral without modification. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (iv): Conditional exceedance probability.** The conditional probability that intensity $I$ exceeds target level $i^*$ given magnitude $m$ and distance $r$ is [@Kramer1996][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$P\\!\\left[I > i^* \\mid m, r\\right] = 1 - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\frac{\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m, r)}{\\sigma_{\\ln I}}\\right)$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(\\cdot)$ is the standard normal cumulative distribution function. The signed logarithmic residual threshold $\\varepsilon^*(m, r)$, defined as the argument of $\\Phi$ above, quantifies the number of logarithmic standard deviations by which the target intensity $i^*$ lies above the GMPE median for scenario $(m, r)$ [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\varepsilon^*(m, r) \\equiv \\frac{\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m, r)}{\\sigma_{\\ln I}}$$\n\nWhen $i^* = \\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)$ the exceedance probability equals exactly 0.5; exceedance becomes increasingly improbable as $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$ increases. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Epsilon reformulation.** The key step in the epsilon form of the hazard integral is the replacement of the complementary standard normal CDF by the tail integral of the standard normal probability density function $\\phi(\\varepsilon) = (2\\pi)^{-1/2}\\exp(-\\varepsilon^2/2)$. This identity is exact for all finite values of the threshold [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$1 - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\right) = \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon$$\n\nSubstituting this representation converts the two-dimensional hazard integral into a triple integral over the domain $(m, r, \\varepsilon)$, with $\\varepsilon$ integrated from the scenario-dependent lower limit $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$ to $+\\infty$. This formulation makes explicit the convolution structure of the hazard integral: for each $(m, r)$ pair the contribution to $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ is a weighted integral of the standard normal PDF $\\phi(\\varepsilon)$ over the exceedance region, with the joint kernel $f_M(m) \\cdot f_R(r)$ providing the weighting. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Assembly: particularized annual exceedance rate in epsilon form.** Substituting the epsilon tail-integral representation of the exceedance probability and the circular source distance PDF $f_R(r) = 2r/R^2$ into the particularized double integral, and noting $R_{\\min} = 0$ and $R_{\\max} = R$, yields [@HazardKB][@Baker2013]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*) = \\frac{2\\nu_0}{R^2} \\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}} \\int_{R_{\\min}}^{R_{\\max}} r\\; f_M(m) \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon\\; dr\\, dm$$\n\nSubstituting the explicit truncated Gutenberg-Richter density for $f_M(m)$ and factoring all remaining constant terms outside the triple integral produces the final particularized annual exceedance rate in epsilon form [@HazardKB][@Baker2013]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*) = \\frac{2\\,\\nu_0\\, b\\ln 10}{R^2\\!\\left(1 - 10^{-b(M_{\\max}-M_{\\min})}\\right)} \\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}} \\int_{R_{\\min}}^{R_{\\max}} \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\; 10^{-b(m-M_{\\min})}\\; r\\; d\\varepsilon\\; dr\\; dm$$\n\nAll terms outside the triple integral are constants determined by the source parameters $a$, $b$, $M_{\\min}$, $M_{\\max}$, and source radius $R$, with $\\nu_0 = 10^{a - b\\,M_{\\min}}$ substituted explicitly. The core integrand constitutes a convolution of the standard normal PDF $\\phi(\\varepsilon)$ with the unnormalized Gutenberg-Richter magnitude distribution function $10^{-b(m-M_{\\min})}$, modulated by the geometric weight $r$ from the circular source distance PDF. The explicit integration limits are $R_{\\min} = 0$, $R_{\\max} = R$, $M_{\\min}$, and $M_{\\max}$; the lower limit of the $\\varepsilon$ integral, $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$, depends on both $m$ and $r$ through the GMPE median $\\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)$. For a general GMPE, this dependence prevents closed-form evaluation and numerical quadrature over the rectangle $[M_{\\min}, M_{\\max}] \\times [0, R]$ is required. [@Baker2013][@HazardKB]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01KMNfGKCuGD3m55BmwNWBRM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RT6iHVbWt8ty8oX2t2atEk","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/pshaB/sessions/run-20260329_152743/pshaB_test2.compile.research.slot-02.md","content":"## SLOT 2: Canonical hazard equations for MCE and disaggregation\n\nThe particularized hazard integral derived in Slot 1 supports two canonical downstream applications for the single circular areal source: determination of the Maximum Considered Earthquake (MCE) ground-motion intensity and seismic hazard disaggregation. Both formulations are expressed in terms of the normalized standard normal probability density function $\\phi(\\varepsilon)$ associated with the ground motion prediction model, building on the critical residual threshold $\\varepsilon^*(m, r) = [\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)]/\\sigma_{\\ln I}$ introduced in Slot 1. [@HazardKB]\n\n### (a) Maximum Considered Earthquake determination\n\nUnder the Poisson occurrence assumption, the annual exceedance probability (AEP) for intensity $I$ exceeding level $i^*$ is related to the annual exceedance rate by [@HazardKB][@Baker2013]:\n\n$$\\mathrm{AEP} = P_{1\\,\\mathrm{yr}}\\!\\left[I > i^*\\right] = 1 - \\exp\\!\\left[-\\lambda_I(i^*)\\right]$$\n\nFor a target return period $T_R$, inverting this relation yields the corresponding target annual exceedance rate [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}) = -\\ln\\!\\left(1 - \\mathrm{AEP}\\right) = -\\ln\\!\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{T_R}\\right) \\approx \\frac{1}{T_R}, \\qquad T_R \\gg 1$$\n\nThe approximation $-\\ln(1 - 1/T_R) \\approx 1/T_R$ is accurate for large return periods and constitutes the standard engineering approximation in PSHA. The MCE intensity $i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}$ is defined implicitly as the solution to $\\lambda_I(i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}) = -\\ln(1 - 1/T_R)$; because the full hazard integral from Slot 1 requires numerical evaluation for a general GMPE, the inversion of the hazard curve likewise requires numerical methods. [@HazardKB]\n\nFor the MCE scenario conditioned on the maximum magnitude $M_{\\max}$ at a representative source-to-site distance $r$, the conditional exceedance probability in epsilon form is [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$P\\!\\left[I > i^* \\mid M_{\\max}, r\\right] = \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(M_{\\max},\\, r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon = 1 - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\varepsilon^*(M_{\\max}, r)\\right)$$\n\nSetting this probability equal to the target AEP and inverting the standard normal CDF relationship yields the critical MCE epsilon level and the closed-form MCE ground-motion intensity [@McGuire1995][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\varepsilon^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}} = \\Phi^{-1}\\!\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{T_R}\\right)$$\n\n$$i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}} = \\hat{\\eta}_I(M_{\\max}, r)\\cdot\\exp\\!\\left[\\sigma_{\\ln I}\\cdot\\varepsilon^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}\\right]$$\n\nThe quantity $\\varepsilon^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}$ is positive for all practically relevant return periods and increases monotonically with $T_R$, consistent with the expectation that rarer ground-motion levels correspond to above-median GMPE residuals [@McGuire1995][@HazardKB]. The representative distance $r$ requires explicit specification in any application. A geometrically consistent choice is the mean epicentral distance, obtained by integrating $r$ against the circular source distance PDF $f_R(r) = 2r/R^2$ over $[0, R]$ [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\bar{r} = \\int_0^R r\\, f_R(r)\\, dr = \\int_0^R \\frac{2r^2}{R^2}\\, dr = \\frac{2R}{3}$$\n\nAlternatively, the modal magnitude-distance scenario identified by hazard disaggregation provides a hazard-consistent choice; the selection criterion must be stated explicitly in any specific application. [@HazardKB]\n\n### (b) Seismic hazard disaggregation\n\nHazard disaggregation decomposes the total annual exceedance rate $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ into contributions from discrete scenario bins indexed by magnitude $m_k$, distance $r_j$, and optionally GMPE residual $\\varepsilon_\\ell$, producing the conditional distribution over seismic scenarios given that intensity $i^*$ is exceeded. For the single circular source, the critical residual threshold for scenario $(m, r)$ is [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\varepsilon^*(m, r) = \\frac{\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m, r)}{\\sigma_{\\ln I}}$$\n\nExceedance of $i^*$ from scenario $(m, r)$ is possible only when the realized residual $\\varepsilon \\geq \\varepsilon^*(m, r)$; scenarios for which $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$ is large contribute negligibly to the total hazard at intensity level $i^*$. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Two-dimensional M-R disaggregation.** The disaggregation bin rate for magnitude bin $m_k$ and distance bin $r_j$ is obtained by integrating the epsilon-form hazard integrand over the bin domain [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_{k,j}(i^*) = \\frac{2\\nu_0}{R^2} \\int_{m_k}\\!\\int_{r_j} r\\; f_M(m) \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon\\; dr\\, dm$$\n\nThe fractional contribution of bin $(m_k, r_j)$ to the total annual exceedance rate is [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\theta_{k,j} = \\frac{\\lambda_{k,j}(i^*)}{\\lambda_I(i^*)}$$\n\nThe weights satisfy $\\sum_k\\sum_j \\theta_{k,j} = 1$ by construction. The modal scenario $(m_k^*, r_j^*)$ is identified as the bin maximizing $\\theta_{k,j}$, representing the magnitude-distance combination contributing most to hazard at intensity level $i^*$. Both $\\lambda_{k,j}(i^*)$ and $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ require numerical integration for a general GMPE. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Full three-dimensional $(M, R, \\varepsilon)$ disaggregation.** For the full disaggregation including the GMPE residual dimension, the exceedance rate contribution from bin $(m_k, r_j, \\varepsilon_\\ell)$ with $\\varepsilon_\\ell = [\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm lo}, \\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi}]$ is, in canonical epsilon form [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_{k,j,\\ell}(i^*) = \\frac{2\\nu_0}{R^2} \\int_{m_k}\\!\\int_{r_j} r\\, f_M(m) \\int_{\\varepsilon_\\ell} \\mathbf{1}_{\\{\\varepsilon \\geq \\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\}}\\, \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon\\, dr\\, dm$$\n\nThe inner integral over the epsilon bin $[\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm lo}, \\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi}]$ admits closed-form evaluation in terms of the standard normal CDF [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\int_{\\varepsilon_\\ell} \\mathbf{1}_{\\{\\varepsilon \\geq \\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\}}\\,\\phi(\\varepsilon)\\,d\\varepsilon = \\Phi\\!\\left(\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi}\\right) - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\max\\!\\left(\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm lo},\\, \\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\right)\\right), \\quad \\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi} > \\varepsilon^*(m,r)$$\n\nand equals zero when $\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi} \\leq \\varepsilon^*(m,r)$, because no residual in the bin is sufficient to produce exceedance of $i^*$. This closed-form evaluation reduces the three-dimensional integration problem to a double integral over $(m, r)$ within each bin, which requires numerical quadrature for a general GMPE. [@HazardKB]\n\nThe joint conditional disaggregation probability for bin $(m_k, r_j, \\varepsilon_\\ell)$, normalized by the total exceedance rate, is [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\theta_{k,j,\\ell} = P\\!\\left[M \\in m_k,\\, R \\in r_j,\\, \\varepsilon \\in \\varepsilon_\\ell \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\frac{\\lambda_{k,j,\\ell}(i^*)}{\\lambda_I(i^*)}$$\n\nThe completeness condition $\\sum_k\\sum_j\\sum_\\ell \\theta_{k,j,\\ell} = 1$ holds by construction. Marginal conditional distributions over magnitude, distance, and residual are obtained by summing $\\theta_{k,j,\\ell}$ over the remaining bin indices [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$P\\!\\left[M \\in m_k \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\sum_{j}\\sum_{\\ell}\\, \\theta_{k,j,\\ell}, \\qquad P\\!\\left[R \\in r_j \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\sum_{k}\\sum_{\\ell}\\, \\theta_{k,j,\\ell}, \\qquad P\\!\\left[\\varepsilon \\in \\varepsilon_\\ell \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\sum_{k}\\sum_{j}\\, \\theta_{k,j,\\ell}$$\n\nFor the circular source, the distance PDF $f_R(r) = 2r/R^2$ concentrates probability at larger epicentral distances, while GMPE attenuation reduces exceedance probability with increasing distance; the marginal conditional distribution $P[R \\in r_j \\mid I > i^*]$ reflects the competition between these effects and is generally peaked at some intermediate distance, requiring numerical evaluation for any specific GMPE. [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17651,"cache_read_input_tokens":41926,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":17651,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aaa7868a-6dc9-476b-8d17-65e6c005c5fe","uuid":"a3aff5b2-856e-4b04-a687-1d9e5bf0eb06"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RT6iHVbWt8ty8oX2t2atEk","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/pshaB/sessions/run-20260329_152743/pshaB_test2.compile.research.slot-02.md"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aaa7868a-6dc9-476b-8d17-65e6c005c5fe","uuid":"ac7aff8a-2019-4f76-84e6-2e4d828e49d4","timestamp":"2026-03-29T18:34:23.356Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/pshaB/sessions/run-20260329_152743/pshaB_test2.compile.research.slot-02.md","content":"## SLOT 2: Canonical hazard equations for MCE and disaggregation\n\nThe particularized hazard integral derived in Slot 1 supports two canonical downstream applications for the single circular areal source: determination of the Maximum Considered Earthquake (MCE) ground-motion intensity and seismic hazard disaggregation. Both formulations are expressed in terms of the normalized standard normal probability density function $\\phi(\\varepsilon)$ associated with the ground motion prediction model, building on the critical residual threshold $\\varepsilon^*(m, r) = [\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)]/\\sigma_{\\ln I}$ introduced in Slot 1. [@HazardKB]\n\n### (a) Maximum Considered Earthquake determination\n\nUnder the Poisson occurrence assumption, the annual exceedance probability (AEP) for intensity $I$ exceeding level $i^*$ is related to the annual exceedance rate by [@HazardKB][@Baker2013]:\n\n$$\\mathrm{AEP} = P_{1\\,\\mathrm{yr}}\\!\\left[I > i^*\\right] = 1 - \\exp\\!\\left[-\\lambda_I(i^*)\\right]$$\n\nFor a target return period $T_R$, inverting this relation yields the corresponding target annual exceedance rate [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}) = -\\ln\\!\\left(1 - \\mathrm{AEP}\\right) = -\\ln\\!\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{T_R}\\right) \\approx \\frac{1}{T_R}, \\qquad T_R \\gg 1$$\n\nThe approximation $-\\ln(1 - 1/T_R) \\approx 1/T_R$ is accurate for large return periods and constitutes the standard engineering approximation in PSHA. The MCE intensity $i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}$ is defined implicitly as the solution to $\\lambda_I(i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}) = -\\ln(1 - 1/T_R)$; because the full hazard integral from Slot 1 requires numerical evaluation for a general GMPE, the inversion of the hazard curve likewise requires numerical methods. [@HazardKB]\n\nFor the MCE scenario conditioned on the maximum magnitude $M_{\\max}$ at a representative source-to-site distance $r$, the conditional exceedance probability in epsilon form is [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$P\\!\\left[I > i^* \\mid M_{\\max}, r\\right] = \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(M_{\\max},\\, r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon = 1 - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\varepsilon^*(M_{\\max}, r)\\right)$$\n\nSetting this probability equal to the target AEP and inverting the standard normal CDF relationship yields the critical MCE epsilon level and the closed-form MCE ground-motion intensity [@McGuire1995][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\varepsilon^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}} = \\Phi^{-1}\\!\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{T_R}\\right)$$\n\n$$i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}} = \\hat{\\eta}_I(M_{\\max}, r)\\cdot\\exp\\!\\left[\\sigma_{\\ln I}\\cdot\\varepsilon^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}\\right]$$\n\nThe quantity $\\varepsilon^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}$ is positive for all practically relevant return periods and increases monotonically with $T_R$, consistent with the expectation that rarer ground-motion levels correspond to above-median GMPE residuals [@McGuire1995][@HazardKB]. The representative distance $r$ requires explicit specification in any application. A geometrically consistent choice is the mean epicentral distance, obtained by integrating $r$ against the circular source distance PDF $f_R(r) = 2r/R^2$ over $[0, R]$ [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\bar{r} = \\int_0^R r\\, f_R(r)\\, dr = \\int_0^R \\frac{2r^2}{R^2}\\, dr = \\frac{2R}{3}$$\n\nAlternatively, the modal magnitude-distance scenario identified by hazard disaggregation provides a hazard-consistent choice; the selection criterion must be stated explicitly in any specific application. [@HazardKB]\n\n### (b) Seismic hazard disaggregation\n\nHazard disaggregation decomposes the total annual exceedance rate $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ into contributions from discrete scenario bins indexed by magnitude $m_k$, distance $r_j$, and optionally GMPE residual $\\varepsilon_\\ell$, producing the conditional distribution over seismic scenarios given that intensity $i^*$ is exceeded. For the single circular source, the critical residual threshold for scenario $(m, r)$ is [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\varepsilon^*(m, r) = \\frac{\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m, r)}{\\sigma_{\\ln I}}$$\n\nExceedance of $i^*$ from scenario $(m, r)$ is possible only when the realized residual $\\varepsilon \\geq \\varepsilon^*(m, r)$; scenarios for which $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$ is large contribute negligibly to the total hazard at intensity level $i^*$. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Two-dimensional M-R disaggregation.** The disaggregation bin rate for magnitude bin $m_k$ and distance bin $r_j$ is obtained by integrating the epsilon-form hazard integrand over the bin domain [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_{k,j}(i^*) = \\frac{2\\nu_0}{R^2} \\int_{m_k}\\!\\int_{r_j} r\\; f_M(m) \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon\\; dr\\, dm$$\n\nThe fractional contribution of bin $(m_k, r_j)$ to the total annual exceedance rate is [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\theta_{k,j} = \\frac{\\lambda_{k,j}(i^*)}{\\lambda_I(i^*)}$$\n\nThe weights satisfy $\\sum_k\\sum_j \\theta_{k,j} = 1$ by construction. The modal scenario $(m_k^*, r_j^*)$ is identified as the bin maximizing $\\theta_{k,j}$, representing the magnitude-distance combination contributing most to hazard at intensity level $i^*$. Both $\\lambda_{k,j}(i^*)$ and $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ require numerical integration for a general GMPE. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Full three-dimensional $(M, R, \\varepsilon)$ disaggregation.** For the full disaggregation including the GMPE residual dimension, the exceedance rate contribution from bin $(m_k, r_j, \\varepsilon_\\ell)$ with $\\varepsilon_\\ell = [\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm lo}, \\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi}]$ is, in canonical epsilon form [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_{k,j,\\ell}(i^*) = \\frac{2\\nu_0}{R^2} \\int_{m_k}\\!\\int_{r_j} r\\, f_M(m) \\int_{\\varepsilon_\\ell} \\mathbf{1}_{\\{\\varepsilon \\geq \\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\}}\\, \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon\\, dr\\, dm$$\n\nThe inner integral over the epsilon bin $[\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm lo}, \\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi}]$ admits closed-form evaluation in terms of the standard normal CDF [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\int_{\\varepsilon_\\ell} \\mathbf{1}_{\\{\\varepsilon \\geq \\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\}}\\,\\phi(\\varepsilon)\\,d\\varepsilon = \\Phi\\!\\left(\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi}\\right) - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\max\\!\\left(\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm lo},\\, \\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\right)\\right), \\quad \\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi} > \\varepsilon^*(m,r)$$\n\nand equals zero when $\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi} \\leq \\varepsilon^*(m,r)$, because no residual in the bin is sufficient to produce exceedance of $i^*$. This closed-form evaluation reduces the three-dimensional integration problem to a double integral over $(m, r)$ within each bin, which requires numerical quadrature for a general GMPE. [@HazardKB]\n\nThe joint conditional disaggregation probability for bin $(m_k, r_j, \\varepsilon_\\ell)$, normalized by the total exceedance rate, is [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\theta_{k,j,\\ell} = P\\!\\left[M \\in m_k,\\, R \\in r_j,\\, \\varepsilon \\in \\varepsilon_\\ell \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\frac{\\lambda_{k,j,\\ell}(i^*)}{\\lambda_I(i^*)}$$\n\nThe completeness condition $\\sum_k\\sum_j\\sum_\\ell \\theta_{k,j,\\ell} = 1$ holds by construction. Marginal conditional distributions over magnitude, distance, and residual are obtained by summing $\\theta_{k,j,\\ell}$ over the remaining bin indices [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$P\\!\\left[M \\in m_k \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\sum_{j}\\sum_{\\ell}\\, \\theta_{k,j,\\ell}, \\qquad P\\!\\left[R \\in r_j \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\sum_{k}\\sum_{\\ell}\\, \\theta_{k,j,\\ell}, \\qquad P\\!\\left[\\varepsilon \\in \\varepsilon_\\ell \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\sum_{k}\\sum_{j}\\, \\theta_{k,j,\\ell}$$\n\nFor the circular source, the distance PDF $f_R(r) = 2r/R^2$ concentrates probability at larger epicentral distances, while GMPE attenuation reduces exceedance probability with increasing distance; the marginal conditional distribution $P[R \\in r_j \\mid I > i^*]$ reflects the competition between these effects and is generally peaked at some intermediate distance, requiring numerical evaluation for any specific GMPE. [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01D35449DC9VbsCx3UULHCJ4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"PHASE_PROGRESS: assembly :: reading_slot_outputs\n\nPHASE_PROGRESS: assembly :: writing_final_outputs"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":84619,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":84619,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aaa7868a-6dc9-476b-8d17-65e6c005c5fe","uuid":"35469cc1-8f67-4cf0-9292-313bf12ade78"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01D35449DC9VbsCx3UULHCJ4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AxSDaVfLqs2SqwxNA58wUC","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/pshaB/sessions/run-20260329_152743/pshaB_test2.compile.research.md","content":"# Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis: Single Circular Areal Source\n\n## SLOT 1: Derivation of the particularized annual exceedance rate in epsilon form\n\nThe general PSHA hazard integral is particularized to a single circular areal source of radius $R$ centered on the study site. Seismicity within the source is spatially uniform, and contributions from beyond radius $R$ are negligible; the distance integration domain reduces to $[0, R]$. The source is characterized by Gutenberg-Richter parameters $a$ and $b$, engineering minimum magnitude $M_{\\min}$, and maximum magnitude $M_{\\max}$. Four components of the integrand require explicit specification: the annual occurrence rate $\\nu_0$, the truncated Gutenberg-Richter magnitude probability density function $f_M(m)$, the conditional ground-motion exceedance probability $P[I > i^* \\mid m, r]$, and the distance probability density function $f_R(r)$ appropriate to the circular source geometry. No specific ground motion prediction equation is assumed; the ground-motion intensity $I$ is treated as lognormally distributed with median $\\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)$ and logarithmic standard deviation $\\sigma_{\\ln I}$ under reference rock conditions throughout. [@Baker2013][@Kramer1996][@HazardKB]\n\nReducing the general multi-source hazard sum to the single circular source, the particularized annual exceedance rate takes the form [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*) = \\nu_0 \\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}} \\int_{R_{\\min}}^{R_{\\max}} P\\!\\left[I > i^* \\mid m, r\\right] f_M(m)\\, f_R(r)\\, dr\\, dm$$\n\nwhere $R_{\\min} = 0$ and $R_{\\max} = R$ are the inner and outer radial limits imposed by the circular source geometry centered on the site. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (i): Annual occurrence rate.** The Gutenberg-Richter recurrence relation $\\log_{10} N(M) = a - bM$, where $N(M)$ is the mean annual number of earthquakes at or above magnitude $M$, is evaluated at the engineering minimum magnitude $M_{\\min}$ to yield the annual occurrence rate [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\nu_0 = 10^{a - b\\,M_{\\min}}$$\n\nThe parameters $a$ and $b$ are the activity rate and slope of the Gutenberg-Richter relation for the source. The rate $\\nu_0$ counts the mean annual number of seismically relevant events on the source and is factored outside all subsequent integrals. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (ii): Truncated Gutenberg-Richter magnitude PDF.** The magnitude density is obtained by normalizing the Gutenberg-Richter density over the engineering support $[M_{\\min}, M_{\\max}]$. Evaluating the normalization denominator and canceling common factors yields the closed-form truncated Gutenberg-Richter PDF [@Kramer1996][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$f_M(m) = \\frac{b\\ln 10\\; 10^{-b(m - M_{\\min})}}{1 - 10^{-b(M_{\\max}-M_{\\min})}}, \\qquad M_{\\min} \\leq m \\leq M_{\\max}$$\n\nThe denominator $1 - 10^{-b(M_{\\max}-M_{\\min})}$ is the normalization constant ensuring that $f_M(m)$ integrates to unity over $[M_{\\min}, M_{\\max}]$. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (iii): Distance PDF for the circular source.** For a site at the center of a circular areal source with spatially uniform seismicity, the probability that an earthquake originates within the annular ring at distance $r$ from the site is proportional to the ring area $2\\pi r\\,dr$ divided by the total disk area $\\pi R^2$, yielding [@Baker2013][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$f_R(r) = \\frac{2r}{R^2}, \\qquad 0 \\leq r \\leq R$$\n\nThe density is monotonically increasing in $r$, reflecting the geometric fact that more source area lies at larger epicentral distances from the central site. Under the uniform seismicity assumption, earthquake locations are spatially independent of earthquake magnitude, so the conditional distance distribution given magnitude reduces to the unconditional form: $f_{R|M}(r|m) = f_R(r)$. This independence property carries through the full magnitude-distance integral without modification. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (iv): Conditional exceedance probability.** The conditional probability that intensity $I$ exceeds target level $i^*$ given magnitude $m$ and distance $r$ is [@Kramer1996][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$P\\!\\left[I > i^* \\mid m, r\\right] = 1 - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\frac{\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m, r)}{\\sigma_{\\ln I}}\\right)$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(\\cdot)$ is the standard normal cumulative distribution function. The signed logarithmic residual threshold $\\varepsilon^*(m, r)$, defined as the argument of $\\Phi$ above, quantifies the number of logarithmic standard deviations by which the target intensity $i^*$ lies above the GMPE median for scenario $(m, r)$ [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\varepsilon^*(m, r) \\equiv \\frac{\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m, r)}{\\sigma_{\\ln I}}$$\n\nWhen $i^* = \\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)$ the exceedance probability equals exactly 0.5; exceedance becomes increasingly improbable as $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$ increases. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Epsilon reformulation.** The key step in the epsilon form of the hazard integral is the replacement of the complementary standard normal CDF by the tail integral of the standard normal probability density function $\\phi(\\varepsilon) = (2\\pi)^{-1/2}\\exp(-\\varepsilon^2/2)$. This identity is exact for all finite values of the threshold [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$1 - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\right) = \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon$$\n\nSubstituting this representation converts the two-dimensional hazard integral into a triple integral over the domain $(m, r, \\varepsilon)$, with $\\varepsilon$ integrated from the scenario-dependent lower limit $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$ to $+\\infty$. This formulation makes explicit the convolution structure of the hazard integral: for each $(m, r)$ pair the contribution to $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ is a weighted integral of the standard normal PDF $\\phi(\\varepsilon)$ over the exceedance region, with the joint kernel $f_M(m) \\cdot f_R(r)$ providing the weighting. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Assembly: particularized annual exceedance rate in epsilon form.** Substituting the epsilon tail-integral representation of the exceedance probability and the circular source distance PDF $f_R(r) = 2r/R^2$ into the particularized double integral, and noting $R_{\\min} = 0$ and $R_{\\max} = R$, yields [@HazardKB][@Baker2013]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*) = \\frac{2\\nu_0}{R^2} \\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}} \\int_{R_{\\min}}^{R_{\\max}} r\\; f_M(m) \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon\\; dr\\, dm$$\n\nSubstituting the explicit truncated Gutenberg-Richter density for $f_M(m)$ and factoring all remaining constant terms outside the triple integral produces the final particularized annual exceedance rate in epsilon form [@HazardKB][@Baker2013]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*) = \\frac{2\\,\\nu_0\\, b\\ln 10}{R^2\\!\\left(1 - 10^{-b(M_{\\max}-M_{\\min})}\\right)} \\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}} \\int_{R_{\\min}}^{R_{\\max}} \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\; 10^{-b(m-M_{\\min})}\\; r\\; d\\varepsilon\\; dr\\; dm$$\n\nAll terms outside the triple integral are constants determined by the source parameters $a$, $b$, $M_{\\min}$, $M_{\\max}$, and source radius $R$, with $\\nu_0 = 10^{a - b\\,M_{\\min}}$ substituted explicitly. The core integrand constitutes a convolution of the standard normal PDF $\\phi(\\varepsilon)$ with the unnormalized Gutenberg-Richter magnitude distribution function $10^{-b(m-M_{\\min})}$, modulated by the geometric weight $r$ from the circular source distance PDF. The explicit integration limits are $R_{\\min} = 0$, $R_{\\max} = R$, $M_{\\min}$, and $M_{\\max}$; the lower limit of the $\\varepsilon$ integral, $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$, depends on both $m$ and $r$ through the GMPE median $\\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)$. For a general GMPE, this dependence prevents closed-form evaluation and numerical quadrature over the rectangle $[M_{\\min}, M_{\\max}] \\times [0, R]$ is required. [@Baker2013][@HazardKB]\n\n---\n\n## SLOT 2: Canonical hazard equations for MCE and disaggregation\n\nThe particularized hazard integral established in Slot 1 supports two canonical downstream applications for the single circular areal source: determination of the Maximum Considered Earthquake (MCE) ground-motion intensity and seismic hazard disaggregation. Both formulations are expressed in terms of the normalized standard normal probability density function $\\phi(\\varepsilon)$ associated with the ground motion prediction model, building on the critical residual threshold $\\varepsilon^*(m, r) = [\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)]/\\sigma_{\\ln I}$ introduced in Slot 1. [@HazardKB]\n\n### (a) Maximum Considered Earthquake determination\n\nUnder the Poisson occurrence assumption, the annual exceedance probability (AEP) for intensity $I$ exceeding level $i^*$ is related to the annual exceedance rate by [@HazardKB][@Baker2013]:\n\n$$\\mathrm{AEP} = P_{1\\,\\mathrm{yr}}\\!\\left[I > i^*\\right] = 1 - \\exp\\!\\left[-\\lambda_I(i^*)\\right]$$\n\nFor a target return period $T_R$, inverting this relation yields the corresponding target annual exceedance rate [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}) = -\\ln\\!\\left(1 - \\mathrm{AEP}\\right) = -\\ln\\!\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{T_R}\\right) \\approx \\frac{1}{T_R}, \\qquad T_R \\gg 1$$\n\nThe approximation $-\\ln(1 - 1/T_R) \\approx 1/T_R$ is accurate for large return periods and constitutes the standard engineering approximation in PSHA. The MCE intensity $i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}$ is defined implicitly as the solution to $\\lambda_I(i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}) = -\\ln(1 - 1/T_R)$; because the full hazard integral from Slot 1 requires numerical evaluation for a general GMPE, the inversion of the hazard curve likewise requires numerical methods. [@HazardKB]\n\nFor the MCE scenario conditioned on the maximum magnitude $M_{\\max}$ at a representative source-to-site distance $r$, the conditional exceedance probability in epsilon form is [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$P\\!\\left[I > i^* \\mid M_{\\max}, r\\right] = \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(M_{\\max},\\, r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon = 1 - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\varepsilon^*(M_{\\max}, r)\\right)$$\n\nSetting this probability equal to the target AEP and inverting the standard normal CDF relationship yields the critical MCE epsilon level and the closed-form MCE ground-motion intensity [@McGuire1995][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\varepsilon^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}} = \\Phi^{-1}\\!\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{T_R}\\right)$$\n\n$$i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}} = \\hat{\\eta}_I(M_{\\max}, r)\\cdot\\exp\\!\\left[\\sigma_{\\ln I}\\cdot\\varepsilon^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}\\right]$$\n\nThe quantity $\\varepsilon^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}$ is positive for all practically relevant return periods and increases monotonically with $T_R$, consistent with the expectation that rarer ground-motion levels correspond to above-median GMPE residuals [@McGuire1995][@HazardKB]. The representative distance $r$ requires explicit specification in any application. A geometrically consistent choice is the mean epicentral distance, obtained by integrating $r$ against the circular source distance PDF $f_R(r) = 2r/R^2$ over $[0, R]$ [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\bar{r} = \\int_0^R r\\, f_R(r)\\, dr = \\int_0^R \\frac{2r^2}{R^2}\\, dr = \\frac{2R}{3}$$\n\nAlternatively, the modal magnitude-distance scenario identified by hazard disaggregation provides a hazard-consistent choice; the selection criterion must be stated explicitly in any specific application. [@HazardKB]\n\n### (b) Seismic hazard disaggregation\n\nHazard disaggregation decomposes the total annual exceedance rate $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ into contributions from discrete scenario bins indexed by magnitude $m_k$, distance $r_j$, and optionally GMPE residual $\\varepsilon_\\ell$, producing the conditional distribution over seismic scenarios given that intensity $i^*$ is exceeded. For the single circular source, the critical residual threshold for scenario $(m, r)$ is [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\varepsilon^*(m, r) = \\frac{\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m, r)}{\\sigma_{\\ln I}}$$\n\nExceedance of $i^*$ from scenario $(m, r)$ is possible only when the realized residual $\\varepsilon \\geq \\varepsilon^*(m, r)$; scenarios for which $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$ is large contribute negligibly to the total hazard at intensity level $i^*$. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Two-dimensional M-R disaggregation.** The disaggregation bin rate for magnitude bin $m_k$ and distance bin $r_j$ is obtained by integrating the epsilon-form hazard integrand over the bin domain [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_{k,j}(i^*) = \\frac{2\\nu_0}{R^2} \\int_{m_k}\\!\\int_{r_j} r\\; f_M(m) \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon\\; dr\\, dm$$\n\nThe fractional contribution of bin $(m_k, r_j)$ to the total annual exceedance rate is [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\theta_{k,j} = \\frac{\\lambda_{k,j}(i^*)}{\\lambda_I(i^*)}$$\n\nThe weights satisfy $\\sum_k\\sum_j \\theta_{k,j} = 1$ by construction. The modal scenario $(m_k^*, r_j^*)$ is identified as the bin maximizing $\\theta_{k,j}$, representing the magnitude-distance combination contributing most to hazard at intensity level $i^*$. Both $\\lambda_{k,j}(i^*)$ and $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ require numerical integration for a general GMPE. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Full three-dimensional $(M, R, \\varepsilon)$ disaggregation.** For the full disaggregation including the GMPE residual dimension, the exceedance rate contribution from bin $(m_k, r_j, \\varepsilon_\\ell)$ with $\\varepsilon_\\ell = [\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm lo}, \\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi}]$ is, in canonical epsilon form [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_{k,j,\\ell}(i^*) = \\frac{2\\nu_0}{R^2} \\int_{m_k}\\!\\int_{r_j} r\\, f_M(m) \\int_{\\varepsilon_\\ell} \\mathbf{1}_{\\{\\varepsilon \\geq \\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\}}\\, \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon\\, dr\\, dm$$\n\nThe inner integral over the epsilon bin $[\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm lo}, \\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi}]$ admits closed-form evaluation in terms of the standard normal CDF [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\int_{\\varepsilon_\\ell} \\mathbf{1}_{\\{\\varepsilon \\geq \\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\}}\\,\\phi(\\varepsilon)\\,d\\varepsilon = \\Phi\\!\\left(\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi}\\right) - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\max\\!\\left(\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm lo},\\, \\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\right)\\right), \\quad \\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi} > \\varepsilon^*(m,r)$$\n\nand equals zero when $\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi} \\leq \\varepsilon^*(m,r)$, because no residual in the bin is sufficient to produce exceedance of $i^*$. This closed-form evaluation reduces the three-dimensional integration problem to a double integral over $(m, r)$ within each bin, which requires numerical quadrature for a general GMPE. [@HazardKB]\n\nThe joint conditional disaggregation probability for bin $(m_k, r_j, \\varepsilon_\\ell)$, normalized by the total exceedance rate, is [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\theta_{k,j,\\ell} = P\\!\\left[M \\in m_k,\\, R \\in r_j,\\, \\varepsilon \\in \\varepsilon_\\ell \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\frac{\\lambda_{k,j,\\ell}(i^*)}{\\lambda_I(i^*)}$$\n\nThe completeness condition $\\sum_k\\sum_j\\sum_\\ell \\theta_{k,j,\\ell} = 1$ holds by construction. Marginal conditional distributions over magnitude, distance, and residual are obtained by summing $\\theta_{k,j,\\ell}$ over the remaining bin indices [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$P\\!\\left[M \\in m_k \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\sum_{j}\\sum_{\\ell}\\, \\theta_{k,j,\\ell}, \\qquad P\\!\\left[R \\in r_j \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\sum_{k}\\sum_{\\ell}\\, \\theta_{k,j,\\ell}, \\qquad P\\!\\left[\\varepsilon \\in \\varepsilon_\\ell \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\sum_{k}\\sum_{j}\\, \\theta_{k,j,\\ell}$$\n\nFor the circular source, the distance PDF $f_R(r) = 2r/R^2$ concentrates probability at larger epicentral distances, while GMPE attenuation reduces exceedance probability with increasing distance; the marginal conditional distribution $P[R \\in r_j \\mid I > i^*]$ reflects the competition between these effects and is generally peaked at some intermediate distance, requiring numerical evaluation for any specific GMPE. [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":84619,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":84619,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aaa7868a-6dc9-476b-8d17-65e6c005c5fe","uuid":"3fdf3e24-b0a1-49c1-9b56-e609fa254ff5"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AxSDaVfLqs2SqwxNA58wUC","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/pshaB/sessions/run-20260329_152743/pshaB_test2.compile.research.md"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aaa7868a-6dc9-476b-8d17-65e6c005c5fe","uuid":"a07c3fed-4e74-4dd0-bb3c-c5b9eda42717","timestamp":"2026-03-29T18:35:41.171Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/pshaB/sessions/run-20260329_152743/pshaB_test2.compile.research.md","content":"# Probabilistic Seismic Hazard Analysis: Single Circular Areal Source\n\n## SLOT 1: Derivation of the particularized annual exceedance rate in epsilon form\n\nThe general PSHA hazard integral is particularized to a single circular areal source of radius $R$ centered on the study site. Seismicity within the source is spatially uniform, and contributions from beyond radius $R$ are negligible; the distance integration domain reduces to $[0, R]$. The source is characterized by Gutenberg-Richter parameters $a$ and $b$, engineering minimum magnitude $M_{\\min}$, and maximum magnitude $M_{\\max}$. Four components of the integrand require explicit specification: the annual occurrence rate $\\nu_0$, the truncated Gutenberg-Richter magnitude probability density function $f_M(m)$, the conditional ground-motion exceedance probability $P[I > i^* \\mid m, r]$, and the distance probability density function $f_R(r)$ appropriate to the circular source geometry. No specific ground motion prediction equation is assumed; the ground-motion intensity $I$ is treated as lognormally distributed with median $\\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)$ and logarithmic standard deviation $\\sigma_{\\ln I}$ under reference rock conditions throughout. [@Baker2013][@Kramer1996][@HazardKB]\n\nReducing the general multi-source hazard sum to the single circular source, the particularized annual exceedance rate takes the form [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*) = \\nu_0 \\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}} \\int_{R_{\\min}}^{R_{\\max}} P\\!\\left[I > i^* \\mid m, r\\right] f_M(m)\\, f_R(r)\\, dr\\, dm$$\n\nwhere $R_{\\min} = 0$ and $R_{\\max} = R$ are the inner and outer radial limits imposed by the circular source geometry centered on the site. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (i): Annual occurrence rate.** The Gutenberg-Richter recurrence relation $\\log_{10} N(M) = a - bM$, where $N(M)$ is the mean annual number of earthquakes at or above magnitude $M$, is evaluated at the engineering minimum magnitude $M_{\\min}$ to yield the annual occurrence rate [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\nu_0 = 10^{a - b\\,M_{\\min}}$$\n\nThe parameters $a$ and $b$ are the activity rate and slope of the Gutenberg-Richter relation for the source. The rate $\\nu_0$ counts the mean annual number of seismically relevant events on the source and is factored outside all subsequent integrals. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (ii): Truncated Gutenberg-Richter magnitude PDF.** The magnitude density is obtained by normalizing the Gutenberg-Richter density over the engineering support $[M_{\\min}, M_{\\max}]$. Evaluating the normalization denominator and canceling common factors yields the closed-form truncated Gutenberg-Richter PDF [@Kramer1996][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$f_M(m) = \\frac{b\\ln 10\\; 10^{-b(m - M_{\\min})}}{1 - 10^{-b(M_{\\max}-M_{\\min})}}, \\qquad M_{\\min} \\leq m \\leq M_{\\max}$$\n\nThe denominator $1 - 10^{-b(M_{\\max}-M_{\\min})}$ is the normalization constant ensuring that $f_M(m)$ integrates to unity over $[M_{\\min}, M_{\\max}]$. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (iii): Distance PDF for the circular source.** For a site at the center of a circular areal source with spatially uniform seismicity, the probability that an earthquake originates within the annular ring at distance $r$ from the site is proportional to the ring area $2\\pi r\\,dr$ divided by the total disk area $\\pi R^2$, yielding [@Baker2013][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$f_R(r) = \\frac{2r}{R^2}, \\qquad 0 \\leq r \\leq R$$\n\nThe density is monotonically increasing in $r$, reflecting the geometric fact that more source area lies at larger epicentral distances from the central site. Under the uniform seismicity assumption, earthquake locations are spatially independent of earthquake magnitude, so the conditional distance distribution given magnitude reduces to the unconditional form: $f_{R|M}(r|m) = f_R(r)$. This independence property carries through the full magnitude-distance integral without modification. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Component (iv): Conditional exceedance probability.** The conditional probability that intensity $I$ exceeds target level $i^*$ given magnitude $m$ and distance $r$ is [@Kramer1996][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$P\\!\\left[I > i^* \\mid m, r\\right] = 1 - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\frac{\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m, r)}{\\sigma_{\\ln I}}\\right)$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(\\cdot)$ is the standard normal cumulative distribution function. The signed logarithmic residual threshold $\\varepsilon^*(m, r)$, defined as the argument of $\\Phi$ above, quantifies the number of logarithmic standard deviations by which the target intensity $i^*$ lies above the GMPE median for scenario $(m, r)$ [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\varepsilon^*(m, r) \\equiv \\frac{\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m, r)}{\\sigma_{\\ln I}}$$\n\nWhen $i^* = \\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)$ the exceedance probability equals exactly 0.5; exceedance becomes increasingly improbable as $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$ increases. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Epsilon reformulation.** The key step in the epsilon form of the hazard integral is the replacement of the complementary standard normal CDF by the tail integral of the standard normal probability density function $\\phi(\\varepsilon) = (2\\pi)^{-1/2}\\exp(-\\varepsilon^2/2)$. This identity is exact for all finite values of the threshold [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$1 - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\right) = \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon$$\n\nSubstituting this representation converts the two-dimensional hazard integral into a triple integral over the domain $(m, r, \\varepsilon)$, with $\\varepsilon$ integrated from the scenario-dependent lower limit $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$ to $+\\infty$. This formulation makes explicit the convolution structure of the hazard integral: for each $(m, r)$ pair the contribution to $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ is a weighted integral of the standard normal PDF $\\phi(\\varepsilon)$ over the exceedance region, with the joint kernel $f_M(m) \\cdot f_R(r)$ providing the weighting. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Assembly: particularized annual exceedance rate in epsilon form.** Substituting the epsilon tail-integral representation of the exceedance probability and the circular source distance PDF $f_R(r) = 2r/R^2$ into the particularized double integral, and noting $R_{\\min} = 0$ and $R_{\\max} = R$, yields [@HazardKB][@Baker2013]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*) = \\frac{2\\nu_0}{R^2} \\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}} \\int_{R_{\\min}}^{R_{\\max}} r\\; f_M(m) \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon\\; dr\\, dm$$\n\nSubstituting the explicit truncated Gutenberg-Richter density for $f_M(m)$ and factoring all remaining constant terms outside the triple integral produces the final particularized annual exceedance rate in epsilon form [@HazardKB][@Baker2013]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*) = \\frac{2\\,\\nu_0\\, b\\ln 10}{R^2\\!\\left(1 - 10^{-b(M_{\\max}-M_{\\min})}\\right)} \\int_{M_{\\min}}^{M_{\\max}} \\int_{R_{\\min}}^{R_{\\max}} \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\; 10^{-b(m-M_{\\min})}\\; r\\; d\\varepsilon\\; dr\\; dm$$\n\nAll terms outside the triple integral are constants determined by the source parameters $a$, $b$, $M_{\\min}$, $M_{\\max}$, and source radius $R$, with $\\nu_0 = 10^{a - b\\,M_{\\min}}$ substituted explicitly. The core integrand constitutes a convolution of the standard normal PDF $\\phi(\\varepsilon)$ with the unnormalized Gutenberg-Richter magnitude distribution function $10^{-b(m-M_{\\min})}$, modulated by the geometric weight $r$ from the circular source distance PDF. The explicit integration limits are $R_{\\min} = 0$, $R_{\\max} = R$, $M_{\\min}$, and $M_{\\max}$; the lower limit of the $\\varepsilon$ integral, $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$, depends on both $m$ and $r$ through the GMPE median $\\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)$. For a general GMPE, this dependence prevents closed-form evaluation and numerical quadrature over the rectangle $[M_{\\min}, M_{\\max}] \\times [0, R]$ is required. [@Baker2013][@HazardKB]\n\n---\n\n## SLOT 2: Canonical hazard equations for MCE and disaggregation\n\nThe particularized hazard integral established in Slot 1 supports two canonical downstream applications for the single circular areal source: determination of the Maximum Considered Earthquake (MCE) ground-motion intensity and seismic hazard disaggregation. Both formulations are expressed in terms of the normalized standard normal probability density function $\\phi(\\varepsilon)$ associated with the ground motion prediction model, building on the critical residual threshold $\\varepsilon^*(m, r) = [\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m,r)]/\\sigma_{\\ln I}$ introduced in Slot 1. [@HazardKB]\n\n### (a) Maximum Considered Earthquake determination\n\nUnder the Poisson occurrence assumption, the annual exceedance probability (AEP) for intensity $I$ exceeding level $i^*$ is related to the annual exceedance rate by [@HazardKB][@Baker2013]:\n\n$$\\mathrm{AEP} = P_{1\\,\\mathrm{yr}}\\!\\left[I > i^*\\right] = 1 - \\exp\\!\\left[-\\lambda_I(i^*)\\right]$$\n\nFor a target return period $T_R$, inverting this relation yields the corresponding target annual exceedance rate [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_I(i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}) = -\\ln\\!\\left(1 - \\mathrm{AEP}\\right) = -\\ln\\!\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{T_R}\\right) \\approx \\frac{1}{T_R}, \\qquad T_R \\gg 1$$\n\nThe approximation $-\\ln(1 - 1/T_R) \\approx 1/T_R$ is accurate for large return periods and constitutes the standard engineering approximation in PSHA. The MCE intensity $i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}$ is defined implicitly as the solution to $\\lambda_I(i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}) = -\\ln(1 - 1/T_R)$; because the full hazard integral from Slot 1 requires numerical evaluation for a general GMPE, the inversion of the hazard curve likewise requires numerical methods. [@HazardKB]\n\nFor the MCE scenario conditioned on the maximum magnitude $M_{\\max}$ at a representative source-to-site distance $r$, the conditional exceedance probability in epsilon form is [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$P\\!\\left[I > i^* \\mid M_{\\max}, r\\right] = \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(M_{\\max},\\, r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon = 1 - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\varepsilon^*(M_{\\max}, r)\\right)$$\n\nSetting this probability equal to the target AEP and inverting the standard normal CDF relationship yields the critical MCE epsilon level and the closed-form MCE ground-motion intensity [@McGuire1995][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\varepsilon^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}} = \\Phi^{-1}\\!\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{T_R}\\right)$$\n\n$$i^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}} = \\hat{\\eta}_I(M_{\\max}, r)\\cdot\\exp\\!\\left[\\sigma_{\\ln I}\\cdot\\varepsilon^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}\\right]$$\n\nThe quantity $\\varepsilon^*_{\\mathrm{MCE}}$ is positive for all practically relevant return periods and increases monotonically with $T_R$, consistent with the expectation that rarer ground-motion levels correspond to above-median GMPE residuals [@McGuire1995][@HazardKB]. The representative distance $r$ requires explicit specification in any application. A geometrically consistent choice is the mean epicentral distance, obtained by integrating $r$ against the circular source distance PDF $f_R(r) = 2r/R^2$ over $[0, R]$ [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\bar{r} = \\int_0^R r\\, f_R(r)\\, dr = \\int_0^R \\frac{2r^2}{R^2}\\, dr = \\frac{2R}{3}$$\n\nAlternatively, the modal magnitude-distance scenario identified by hazard disaggregation provides a hazard-consistent choice; the selection criterion must be stated explicitly in any specific application. [@HazardKB]\n\n### (b) Seismic hazard disaggregation\n\nHazard disaggregation decomposes the total annual exceedance rate $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ into contributions from discrete scenario bins indexed by magnitude $m_k$, distance $r_j$, and optionally GMPE residual $\\varepsilon_\\ell$, producing the conditional distribution over seismic scenarios given that intensity $i^*$ is exceeded. For the single circular source, the critical residual threshold for scenario $(m, r)$ is [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\varepsilon^*(m, r) = \\frac{\\ln i^* - \\ln\\hat{\\eta}_I(m, r)}{\\sigma_{\\ln I}}$$\n\nExceedance of $i^*$ from scenario $(m, r)$ is possible only when the realized residual $\\varepsilon \\geq \\varepsilon^*(m, r)$; scenarios for which $\\varepsilon^*(m,r)$ is large contribute negligibly to the total hazard at intensity level $i^*$. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Two-dimensional M-R disaggregation.** The disaggregation bin rate for magnitude bin $m_k$ and distance bin $r_j$ is obtained by integrating the epsilon-form hazard integrand over the bin domain [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_{k,j}(i^*) = \\frac{2\\nu_0}{R^2} \\int_{m_k}\\!\\int_{r_j} r\\; f_M(m) \\int_{\\varepsilon^*(m,r)}^{+\\infty} \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon\\; dr\\, dm$$\n\nThe fractional contribution of bin $(m_k, r_j)$ to the total annual exceedance rate is [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\theta_{k,j} = \\frac{\\lambda_{k,j}(i^*)}{\\lambda_I(i^*)}$$\n\nThe weights satisfy $\\sum_k\\sum_j \\theta_{k,j} = 1$ by construction. The modal scenario $(m_k^*, r_j^*)$ is identified as the bin maximizing $\\theta_{k,j}$, representing the magnitude-distance combination contributing most to hazard at intensity level $i^*$. Both $\\lambda_{k,j}(i^*)$ and $\\lambda_I(i^*)$ require numerical integration for a general GMPE. [@HazardKB]\n\n**Full three-dimensional $(M, R, \\varepsilon)$ disaggregation.** For the full disaggregation including the GMPE residual dimension, the exceedance rate contribution from bin $(m_k, r_j, \\varepsilon_\\ell)$ with $\\varepsilon_\\ell = [\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm lo}, \\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi}]$ is, in canonical epsilon form [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\lambda_{k,j,\\ell}(i^*) = \\frac{2\\nu_0}{R^2} \\int_{m_k}\\!\\int_{r_j} r\\, f_M(m) \\int_{\\varepsilon_\\ell} \\mathbf{1}_{\\{\\varepsilon \\geq \\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\}}\\, \\phi(\\varepsilon)\\, d\\varepsilon\\, dr\\, dm$$\n\nThe inner integral over the epsilon bin $[\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm lo}, \\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi}]$ admits closed-form evaluation in terms of the standard normal CDF [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\int_{\\varepsilon_\\ell} \\mathbf{1}_{\\{\\varepsilon \\geq \\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\}}\\,\\phi(\\varepsilon)\\,d\\varepsilon = \\Phi\\!\\left(\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi}\\right) - \\Phi\\!\\left(\\max\\!\\left(\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm lo},\\, \\varepsilon^*(m,r)\\right)\\right), \\quad \\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi} > \\varepsilon^*(m,r)$$\n\nand equals zero when $\\varepsilon_\\ell^{\\rm hi} \\leq \\varepsilon^*(m,r)$, because no residual in the bin is sufficient to produce exceedance of $i^*$. This closed-form evaluation reduces the three-dimensional integration problem to a double integral over $(m, r)$ within each bin, which requires numerical quadrature for a general GMPE. [@HazardKB]\n\nThe joint conditional disaggregation probability for bin $(m_k, r_j, \\varepsilon_\\ell)$, normalized by the total exceedance rate, is [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]:\n\n$$\\theta_{k,j,\\ell} = P\\!\\left[M \\in m_k,\\, R \\in r_j,\\, \\varepsilon \\in \\varepsilon_\\ell \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\frac{\\lambda_{k,j,\\ell}(i^*)}{\\lambda_I(i^*)}$$\n\nThe completeness condition $\\sum_k\\sum_j\\sum_\\ell \\theta_{k,j,\\ell} = 1$ holds by construction. Marginal conditional distributions over magnitude, distance, and residual are obtained by summing $\\theta_{k,j,\\ell}$ over the remaining bin indices [@HazardKB]:\n\n$$P\\!\\left[M \\in m_k \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\sum_{j}\\sum_{\\ell}\\, \\theta_{k,j,\\ell}, \\qquad P\\!\\left[R \\in r_j \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\sum_{k}\\sum_{\\ell}\\, \\theta_{k,j,\\ell}, \\qquad P\\!\\left[\\varepsilon \\in \\varepsilon_\\ell \\mid I > i^*\\right] = \\sum_{k}\\sum_{j}\\, \\theta_{k,j,\\ell}$$\n\nFor the circular source, the distance PDF $f_R(r) = 2r/R^2$ concentrates probability at larger epicentral distances, while GMPE attenuation reduces exceedance probability with increasing distance; the marginal conditional distribution $P[R \\in r_j \\mid I > i^*]$ reflects the competition between these effects and is generally peaked at some intermediate distance, requiring numerical evaluation for any specific GMPE. [@BazzurroCornell1999][@HazardKB]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null}}
